# conductor:ui - Conductor's web interface, sandbox plane only.
#
# Two differences from ./Dockerfile, which this pull request does not touch, so
# the development, staging and production builds provably cannot change.
#
# First, the base is pinned. ./Dockerfile says plainly "FROM nginx", with no
# version at all, so what gets built depends entirely on what the registry's
# moving tag points at that day — the same source has produced different images
# on different days, and nobody can tell from the repository which one is
# running.
#
# Second, it is the Alpine variant rather than the Debian one. The Debian base
# ships perl and a far larger package set; Alpine ships neither, which is what
# cleared the bulk of the high and medium findings the scanner had been
# reporting against this image.
#
# Why the base moved again, and why packages are upgraded on top of it.
# The first rebuild on 2026-11-19 pinned nginx:1.29.8-alpine3.23 and came back
# with 22 critical findings, worse than the 18 it replaced — every one an
# operating-system package, and every one with a fix already published. The
# cause was not the choice of Alpine but the age of the base image: it was baked
# before those patches reached the Alpine repositories. Reading the repositories
# directly on 2026-11-19, both the 3.23 and 3.24 branches already carried fixed
# curl 8.23.0-r0, openssl 3.5.9, zlib 1.4-r0 and brotli 1.2.1-r0, while the
# image still held curl 8.17.0, openssl 3.5.6, zlib 1.3.2 and brotli 1.2.0.
#
# Moving the pin forward alone would only buy time, because any base image
# begins ageing the day it is built. Upgrading installed packages during the
# build closes that gap for good: the image always leaves with whatever the
# distribution has published by build time. This costs nothing in traceability,
# because what actually runs on the plane is pinned by content digest in the
# infrastructure repository, not by this tag.
#
# The interface serves static files and proxies to the server; it holds no
# credential and runs no application code of ours, so the base swap carries
# little behavioural risk. The one thing to confirm on the first build is that
# nginx's user is still uid 101 — the plane's chart pins runAsUser to 101 and
# mounts its cache and pid directories as emptyDirs on that assumption
# (k8s/mesta-services/conductor-ui/sandbox/nginx-writable.yaml in the
# infrastructure repository). If Alpine ever moves that uid, pin
# nginx:1.31.9-trixie instead and accept the larger package set.
FROM nginx:1.31.9-alpine3.24
RUN apk upgrade --no-cache
COPY default.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
COPY build/ /usr/share/nginx/html
